section.center
	h1 Simplemenu
	h3 for Reveal.js

section#toc.center
	h4 Table of contents
	p.small (TOC generated by Simplemenu)
	ul.menu

section(data-name="Why?")

	section.center
		h2 Why?

	section.center
		p In Powerpoint you can make slides with a nice menu in which the active menu item is highlighted. That is a lot of work.
		p.small Simplemenu can do the same for you, but automatically. You will want to use this to organize your next presentation in an easy way.

	section.center
		h4 What Simplemenu does
		ul
			li Generates a menu of your sections and stacks
			li Puts the menu in a header or footer
			li And/or on any slide as an agenda or TOC
			li Use the menu to navigate
			li Update the menu state during navigation

	section.center
		p.small Menu items can only be regular horizontal slides or vertical stacks.
		img(data-src=`assets/img/simplemenusetup.png`)
		p.small If you can’t name your stacks (like in Markdown), Simplemenu will use the name of the first slide in the stack.


section(data-name="Set it up")

	section.center
		h2 Set it up
		p It’s easy

	section
		h4 JavaScript
		p.small Download the plugin folder and make a reference to it in the Reveal plugin options:
		p.small (The CSS file is autoloaded, but you can also load it manually if you want to changeit.)
		pre
			code.hljs.html(data-line-numbers).
				&lt;script src="plugin/simplemenu/simplemenu.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
				&lt;script&gt;
				    Reveal.initialize({
				        plugins: [ Simplemenu ]
				    });
				&lt;/script&gt;

		p.small Or install it with npm (#[code npm i reveal.js-simplemenu]) and import it:
		pre
			code.hljs.javascript(data-line-numbers).
				import Simplemenu from 'reveal.js-simplemenu';
				import 'reveal.js-simplemenu/plugin/simplemenu/simplemenu.css';
				Reveal.initialize({
				    plugins: [ Simplemenu ]
				});
		//-



	section
		h4 HTML Markup
		p.small Give your sections a #[code data-name]:
		pre
			code.hljs.html(data-line-numbers="5-10").
				&lt;div class="slides"&gt;
				    &lt;section&gt;
				        //...
				    &lt;/section&gt;
				    &lt;section data-name="Why"&gt;
				        //...
				    &lt;/section&gt;
				    &lt;section data-name="Set it up"&gt;
				        //...
				    &lt;/section&gt;
				    //...
				&lt;/div&gt;
		//-

		p.small Add #[code data-sm="false"] to named sections you don’t want to show in the menu.#[br]Markdown is also supported, see the #[a(href="demo-markdown.html", target="_blank") Markdown] demo.

	section
		h4 HTML Markup
		p.small If your deck has no menubar, then Simplemenu adds one with the markup below, and fills any empty menu in it. If you add #[code barhtml] to your configuration, then yours is used instead.
		pre
			code.hljs.javascript(data-line-numbers="3-6").
				Reveal.initialize({
				    simplemenu: {
				        barhtml: {
				            header: "&lt;nav class='menubar'&gt;&lt;ul class='menu'&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;",
				            footer: ""
				        }
				    },
				    plugins: [ Simplemenu ]
				});
		//-

	section
		h4 HTML Markup
		p.small … or manually:
		pre
			code(data-line-numbers="1,2,3").
				&lt;nav class="menubar"&gt;
				    &lt;ul class="menu"&gt;&lt;/ul&gt; <!--Keep empty-->
				&lt;/nav&gt;
				&lt;div class="slides"&gt;
				    &lt;section&gt;
				        //...
				    &lt;/section&gt;
				    //...
				&lt;/div&gt;
		//-

	section
		h4 HTML Markup
		p.small You can also add a TOC slide:
		pre
			code(data-line-numbers="7").
				&lt;div class="slides"&gt;
				    &lt;section&gt;
				        &lt;h1&gt;My presentation&lt;/h1&gt;
				    &lt;/section&gt;
				    &lt;section&gt;
				        &lt;h2&gt;Agenda&lt;/h2&gt;
				        &lt;ul class="menu"&gt;&lt;/ul&gt; <!--Keep empty-->
				    &lt;/section&gt;
				    &lt;section data-name="What it does"&gt;
				        //...
				    &lt;/section&gt;
				    &lt;section data-name="Set it up"&gt;
				        //...
				    &lt;/section&gt;
				    //...
				&lt;/div&gt;
		//-

	section
		h4 Styling
		p.small Simplemenu adds a few style classes that you can add through Reveal’s #[code data-state].
		pre
			code(data-line-numbers="2,8").
				&lt;div class="slides"&gt;
				    &lt;section data-state="hide-menubar"&gt;
				        &lt;h1&gt;My presentation&lt;/h1&gt;
				    &lt;/section&gt;
				    &lt;section data-name="What it does"&gt;
				        //...
				    &lt;/section&gt;
				    &lt;section data-name="Thank you" data-state="hide-menu"&gt;
				        //...
				    &lt;/section&gt;
				&lt;/div&gt;
		//-
		p.small #[code hide-menubar] hides the menubar on that slide, #[code hide-menu] hides the menu on that slide.#[br]Both will use animation to transition in and out.


	section
		h4 That’s it! 
		p The setup above is the default (auto) way of linking the menu with the sections. 
		p.small There is also a manual way, where the author creates the links in the menu(s) manually. See #[a(href="demo-manual-dataname.html") demo-manual-dataname.html].


section(data-name="Options").center
	section.center
		h2 Options
		.centerblock
			ol.twocol(style="margin-right: 5%")
				li activeclass
				li activeelement
				li barhtml
				li cssautoload
				li csspath
				li flat
				li menubarclass
				li menuclass
				li rtl
				li scale
				li selectby

	- let optionnr = 1

	section
		h4 Option #{optionnr++}: activeclass
		p.small The activeclass option is the class an active menuitem gets.
		pre
			code.hljs.javascript(data-line-numbers="4").
				Reveal.initialize({
					//...
					simplemenu: {
					    activeclass: "active"
					},
					plugins: [ Simplemenu ]
				})
		//-

	section
		h4 Option #{optionnr++}: activeelement
		p.small The activeelement option sets the item that gets the active class.
		pre
			code.hljs.javascript(data-line-numbers="4").
				Reveal.initialize({
				  //...
				  simplemenu: {
				    activeelement: "li"
				  },
				  plugins: [ Simplemenu ]
				})
		//-
		p.small You may want to change it to the anchor inside the li: activeelement: 'a'.

	section
		h4 Option #{optionnr}: barhtml
		p.small If your deck has no menubar, then Simplemenu adds one and fills any empty menu in it. The default markup below uses your #[code menubarclass] and #[code menuclass].
		pre
			code.hljs.javascript(data-line-numbers="4-7").
				Reveal.initialize({
				  //...
				  simplemenu: {
				    barhtml: { 
				      header: "&lt;nav class='menubar'&gt;&lt;ul class='menu'&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;",
				      footer: ""
				    }
				  },
				  plugins: [ Simplemenu ]
				})
		//-

	section
		h4 Option #{optionnr}: barhtml (continued)
		p.small If you write your own #[code barhtml], then Simplemenu uses it exactly as it is. The classes in it have to be the ones you set.
		pre
			code.hljs.javascript(data-line-numbers="4-9").
				Reveal.initialize({
				  //...
				  simplemenu: {
				    menubarclass: "sm-bar",
				    menuclass: "sm-menu",
				    barhtml: {
				      header: "&lt;nav class='sm-bar'&gt;&lt;ul class='sm-menu'&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;"
				    }
				  },
				  plugins: [ Simplemenu ]
				})
		//-

	section
		h4 Option #{optionnr++}: barhtml (continued)
		p.small You can also move the slide number or the controls to your header or footer. If they are nested there manually, or through the #[code barhtml] option, they will then display inside that header or footer.
		pre
			code.hljs.javascript(data-line-numbers="4-7").
				Reveal.initialize({
				  //...
				  simplemenu: {
				    barhtml: { 
				      header: "",
				      footer: "&lt;nav class='menubar'&gt;&lt;ul class='menu'&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;div class='slide-number'&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;"
				    }
				  },
				  plugins: [ Simplemenu ]
				})
		//-

	section
		h4 Option #{optionnr++}: cssautoload
		p.small Simplemenu will load the CSS if this is set to #[code true].
		pre
			code.hljs.js(data-line-numbers="4").
				Reveal.initialize({
				    ...
				    simplemenu: {
				        cssautoload: true
				    },
				    plugins: [ Simplemenu ]
				})
		//-
		p.small If you import reveal.js-simplemenu from npm, you will need to import the CSS file yourself. If you use 'import', then #[code csspath] should be set to #[code false]. If you know the path to the CSS file, you can use the #[code csspath] option and keep #[code cssautoload] set to #[code true].


	section
		h4 Option #{optionnr++}: csspath
		p.small Simplemenu finds and loads its own stylesheet. If it cannot find it, then #[code csspath] is how you say where it is.
		pre
			code.hljs.javascript(data-line-numbers="4").
				Reveal.initialize({
					//...
					simplemenu: {
					    csspath: "plugin/simplemenu/simplemenu.css"
					},
					plugins: [ Simplemenu ]
				})
		//-
		p.small If you import the stylesheet yourself, then set #[code csspath: false]. To change how the menubar looks, use the CSS variables.

	section
		h4 Option #{optionnr++}: flat
		p.small Sometimes you’ll want to limit your presentation to horizontal slides only. To still use ‘chapters’ with several slides, you can use the #[code flat] option. By default, it is set to #[code false], but you can set it to #[code true]. Then, when a data-name is set for a slide, any following slides will keep that menu name. See #[a(href="demo-flat.html", target="_blank") Flat navigation].
		pre
			code.hljs.javascript(data-line-numbers="4").
				Reveal.initialize({
					//...
					simplemenu: {
					    flat: true
					},
					plugins: [ Simplemenu ]
				})
		//-
		p.small To stop inheriting the previous slide menu name, start a new named section, or add #[code data-sm="false"] to your slide.

	section
		h4 Option #{optionnr++}: menubarclass
		p.small The menubarclass option sets the classname of menubars. Change it if another plugin already uses that name. Simplemenu still styles the bar, because it writes #[code simplemenu-bar] onto it as well.
		pre
			code.hljs.javascript(data-line-numbers="4").
				Reveal.initialize({
					//...
					simplemenu: {
					    menubarclass: "menubar"
					},
					plugins: [ Simplemenu ]
				})
		//-
		p.small Simplemenu will show the menubar(s) on all pages. If you do not want to show the menubar on certain pages, use #[code data-state="hide-menubar"] on that section. This behaviour also works when exporting to PDF using the Reveal.js #[code ?print-pdf] option.

	section
		h4 Option #{optionnr++}: menuclass
		p.small The menuclass option sets the classname of the menu. Change it if another plugin already uses that name. Simplemenu still styles the menu, because it writes #[code simplemenu-menu] onto it as well.
		pre
			code.hljs.javascript(data-line-numbers="4").
				Reveal.initialize({
					//...
					simplemenu: {
					    menuclass: "menu"
					},
					plugins: [ Simplemenu ]
				})
		//-
		p.small Simplemenu looks inside this menu for list items (LI's).

	section
		h4 Option #{optionnr++}: rtl
		p.small When rtl (right-to-left) is set in the Reveal.js options, any automatic menu-items in menubars will inherit that, and have a reversed order. If you do not like that behaviour, you can set it to #[code false] in the Simplemenu options.
		pre
			code.hljs.javascript(data-line-numbers="5").
				Reveal.initialize({
					//...
					rtl: true,
					simplemenu: {
					    rtl: true
					},
					plugins: [ Simplemenu ]
				})
		//-
		p.small It is set to be two-thirds of the main scaling.

	section
		h4 Option #{optionnr++}: scale
		p.small When you have a lot of subjects/chapters in your menubar, they might not all fit in a row. You can then tweak the scale in the options. Simplemenu copies the Reveal.js (slide) scaling and adds a #[code scale] option on top of that.
		pre
			code.hljs.javascript(data-line-numbers="4").
				Reveal.initialize({
					//...
					simplemenu: {
					    scale: 0.67
					},
					plugins: [ Simplemenu ]
				})
		//-
		p.small It is set to be two-thirds of the main scaling.

	section
		h4 Option #{optionnr++}: selectby
		p.small #[em This option is only needed when adding a menu manually. ] You then need to link sections to the menu items. The selectby option finds the active slide or stack.
		pre
			code.hljs.javascript(data-line-numbers="4").
				Reveal.initialize({
					//...
					simplemenu: {
					    selectby: "id"
					},
					plugins: [ Simplemenu ]
				})
		//-
		p.small By default it is 'id', but can also be set to 'data-name'. If set to data-name, Simplemenu will compare the text content of the link to the section. Then #[code &lt;a(href="#")&gt;What it does&lt;/a&gt;] will link to #[code &lt;section(data-name="What it does")&gt;&lt;/section&gt;].

section.center(data-name="Demos")
	h2 Demos
	p See all demos on the main #[a(href="https://github.com/martinomagnifico/reveal.js-simplemenu", target="_blank") Simplemenu page].